 ## v3.7.17
 
 Starting with `v3.7.17`, Traefik is built with Go 1.27.
 
 ### GODEBUG TLS Settings
 
 Go 1.27 removed the `tlsrsakex`, `tls10server` and `tls3des` `GODEBUG` settings.
 Setting them in the `GODEBUG` environment variable has no effect anymore.
 
 For the incoming connections, the RSA key exchange and 3DES cipher suites, as well as TLS 1.0 and 1.1,
 can still be enabled with the `cipherSuites` and `minVersion` fields of the [TLS options](../reference/routing-configuration/http/tls/tls-options.md),
 referenced in the TLS configuration of a [router](../reference/routing-configuration/http/routing/router.md) or of an [entrypoint](../reference/install-configuration/entrypoints.md#opt-http-tls-options).
 
 For the connections to the backends, they can still be enabled with the `cipherSuites` and `minVersion` fields of the [ServersTransport](../reference/routing-configuration/http/load-balancing/serverstransport.md#opt-cipherSuites).
 
 Please check out the [go documentation](https://go.dev/doc/go1.27#cryptotlspkgcryptotls) for more details.
 
 ### Maximum Number of Request Header Values
 
 Go 1.27 limits the number of header values a request can carry to 500.
 
 A request exceeding this limit is rejected with a `431 Request Header Fields Too Large` response,
 for HTTP/1 and HTTP/2.
 This limit can be changed with the new [`maxHeaderValueCount`](../reference/install-configuration/entrypoints.md#opt-http-maxHeaderValueCount) entry point option.
 
 As the rejection happens before the routing, the rejected requests are not reported in the access logs.
 
 Please check out the [go documentation](https://go.dev/doc/go1.27#nethttppkgnethttp) for more details.
 
 ---
 
 ## v3.7.16
 
 ### Kubernetes CRD and Ingress-NGINX Providers: Secret references in middlewares
 
 Until `v3.7.16`, the routing configuration of a middleware held the values that the provider read from Kubernetes Secrets.
 
 Starting with `v3.7.16`, it holds a reference to the Secret key instead, in the form `urn:k8s:secret:<secret name>:<key>`.
 Traefik reads the value when it builds the middleware, so the behavior of the middleware does not change.
 The API, the dashboard and the support dump show the references.
 
 ---
 
 ## v3.7.15
 
 ### CONNECT Requests
 
 Starting with `v3.7.15`, Traefik rejects every `CONNECT` request with a `501 Not Implemented` response,
 whatever the HTTP version used by the client, and never forwards it to the backend.
 
 HTTP/1 `CONNECT` requests were already rejected since `v3.7.9`.
 `CONNECT` requests sent over HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 could seem to work in some cases,
 but they were not handled specifically, while a `CONNECT` tunnel requires a dedicated handling to be safe.
 For security reasons, Traefik now rejects them as well.
 
 As the rejection happens before the routing, the rejected requests are not reported in the access logs.
 They are only visible at the `DEBUG` log level.
 
 To tunnel traffic through a forward proxy exposed by Traefik, route it with a [TCP router](../reference/routing-configuration/tcp/routing/router.md) instead.
 
 ### Kubernetes Gateway API Provider: backend protocol
 
 Until `v3.7.15`, the Kubernetes Gateway API provider used HTTPS to reach the backend of an `HTTPRoute`
 when the Service port had no `appProtocol` and was either the port `443` or a port whose name starts with `https`.
 
 Starting with `v3.7.15`, the port number and the port name do not select HTTPS anymore,
 and such a backend is reached with plain HTTP.
 This follows the Gateway API specification, where only the `appProtocol` of the Service port or a `BackendTLSPolicy` selects the protocol used to reach the backend.
 To keep using HTTPS, set the Service port `appProtocol` to `https`, or attach a `BackendTLSPolicy` to the Service.
 
