 ## v3.7.14
 
 ### Ingress-NGINX Provider: generated names
 
 Until `v3.7.14`, the Ingress-NGINX provider replaced the dots of the Ingress and Secret names with dashes
 in the names of the routers, services, serversTransports and TLS options it generates.
 Two resources named `my.ingress` and `my-ingress` thus produced the same names, and one silently overrode the other.
 
 Starting with `v3.7.14`, the resource names are kept as is: the router of an Ingress named `my.ingress`
 is now `default-my.ingress-rule-0-path-0` instead of `default-my-ingress-rule-0-path-0`.
 Dashboards, alerts and API queries relying on the previous names must be updated.
 
 The routers and middlewares generated on the HTTP entry points for an Ingress carrying the `nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-passthrough` annotation are renamed as well.
 They are now named after the Ingress rule and path they come from, `<namespace>-<ingress>-rule-<rule index>-path-<path index>` instead of `<namespace>-<ingress>-<host>-http`.
 
 ### Kubernetes Gateway API Provider: routes that match a request equally well
 
 Until `v3.7.14`, two routes on the same listener could give routers with the very same rule.
 This occurs, for example, with a route that has no host name and a route that has the host name of the listener.
 One of the two routes served the requests, but you could not know which one.
 The route could also change at each reload of the configuration.
 
 Starting with `v3.7.14`, only the route that has precedence serves the requests.
 The specification gives precedence to the older route, then to the first route in alphabetical order by `{namespace}/{name}`.
 Traefik does not create the routers of the other routes.
 Traefik writes a warning in the log, and gives the name of the router that serves the rule.
 
 ### Kubernetes Gateway API Provider: TLSRoute router names
 
 Until `v3.7.14`, a TLSRoute attached to several TLS listeners sharing an entry point gave routers with the same name,
 and only one of them was kept.
 
 Starting with `v3.7.14`, the hash suffix of the TLSRoute router names is also derived from the router rule,
 so the names of all the TLSRoute routers, and of the services derived from them, change.
 Dashboards, alerts and API queries relying on the previous names must be updated.
 
 ### OpenTelemetry Metrics: exponential histograms
 
 Until `v3.7.14`, the `traefik_*_request_duration_seconds` histograms were always exported with explicit buckets,
 even when `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_METRICS_DEFAULT_HISTOGRAM_AGGREGATION` was set to `base2_exponential_bucket_histogram`.
 
 Starting with `v3.7.14`, these histograms follow this environment variable,
 and the `metrics.otlp.explicitBoundaries` option only applies to explicit bucket histograms.
 Dashboards and alerts relying on the bucket series of these histograms must be updated when the exponential aggregation is enabled.
 
